## Empirical Bayes vs. a full multilevel model

| | **Empirical Bayes (this chapter)** | **Multilevel model (Ch. 8, 13)** |
|---|---|---|
| Speed | Instant, closed-form | Seconds to minutes (MCMC) |
| Uncertainty | Point estimate only, by default | Full posterior for every group |
| Assumes | A single, simple distributional shape for the prior | Whatever you specify — more flexible |
| Good for | A quick first look, dashboards, many repeated similar analyses | The version you'd defend to a sceptical stakeholder or publish |

Neither is "more correct" in every situation. A sensible default:
prototype with empirical Bayes because it's instant, then confirm the
conclusion holds with a proper multilevel model before you act on it —
which is exactly what Chapter 13 does, on a closely related problem.

