## Summary {.unnumbered}

::: callout-note
## Today you learned

1.  Raw rates for small groups are noisy in a *predictable* direction —
    they produce the most extreme values.
2.  Empirical Bayes estimates the prior **from the spread of your own
    groups' rates**, then shrinks each group toward it in closed form.
3.  The shrinkage amount is set automatically by how much groups
    genuinely differ versus how much is just noise.
4.  When you don't have enough groups of your own, an **external
    benchmark** can play the same role as the prior — with an explicit,
    checkable choice of how strongly to weight it.
5.  Empirical Bayes is a fast approximation to the full multilevel
    model in the next chapter — prototype with one, confirm with the
    other.
:::

